# Static editorial articles, not browser-rendered JSON, so search engines receive the full content.
ARTICLES=[
{'slug':'wjec-gcse-maths-numeracy-double-award-guide','title':'WJEC GCSE Maths & Numeracy Double Award: A Parent Guide','meta':'Understand the new WJEC GCSE Mathematics and Numeracy Double Award, how to check your child’s qualification and how to plan useful revision.','short':'What changed in Made-for-Wales Maths, why the exact qualification matters and practical ways to begin revision.','category':'WJEC','image':'maths-study.webp','minutes':5,'body':'''<p>Families looking for GCSE Maths tutoring in South Wales may have noticed a change in the qualifications their children are studying. The new <strong>WJEC GCSE Mathematics and Numeracy (Double Award)</strong> is one of the Made-for-Wales qualifications introduced for teaching from September 2025. It is important to check the specific course, year group and tier before choosing revision materials.</p>
<h2 id="check">1. Check the qualification before buying revision books</h2><p>Do not assume that an older “GCSE Mathematics” past paper matches the current assessment. Ask the school for the qualification name or check the student’s course information. WJEC publishes the <a href="https://www.wjec.co.uk/en/qualifications/gcse-mathematics-and-numeracy-double-award-teaching-from-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Mathematics and Numeracy Double Award page</a>, including the specification and sample assessment materials.</p><p>The new course connects numerical fluency, mathematical language, reasoning and practical application. WJEC identifies number, algebra, geometry and measures, and statistics and probability as core mathematical concepts. For a learner, this means practice should include explanation and interpretation, not only repetitive calculations.</p>
<h2 id="baseline">2. Find the earliest gap, not just the hardest question</h2><p>Suppose a student struggles to solve an algebra problem. It might be because of negative numbers, multiplying fractions, rearranging equations or understanding what a letter represents. A good tutoring session works back to the missing skill and then returns to the original problem.</p><p>One useful approach is to choose eight to ten questions from mixed topics and ask the student to explain how they worked. An incorrect answer is useful evidence. So is an answer they get right by copying a memorised pattern they cannot explain.</p>
<h2 id="revision">3. Combine short revision sessions with questions</h2><p>A practical weekly routine can include one short review of an older topic, a focused explanation of a difficult idea, several independent questions and a quick reflection. Students benefit from spaced practice more than a last-minute rush through entire chapters.</p><ul><li><strong>Number:</strong> fractions, percentages and unit conversions.</li><li><strong>Algebra:</strong> expressions, equations and interpreting graphs.</li><li><strong>Geometry:</strong> measures, angles, area and reasoning.</li><li><strong>Statistics:</strong> representing and interpreting data.</li></ul><p>Use the student’s actual specification to decide which examples and styles of assessment are most relevant.</p>
<h2 id="help">4. When a tutor could help</h2><p>A <a href="../maths-tutoring-south-wales.html">Maths tutor</a> can be helpful when the learner repeatedly makes the same error, avoids questions or cannot explain why an answer is correct. The goal should be to build independent understanding, not simply finish a worksheet during the session.</p><p>South Wales Tutoring provides one-to-one online Maths tuition for students in Cardiff, Swansea, Newport, Bridgend and wider South Wales. Include the WJEC qualification, tier and current topics when you <a href="../contact.html?enquiry_subject=Maths&level=GCSE">enquire about GCSE Maths tutoring</a>.</p>
<div class="article-callout"><strong>Reliable reference:</strong> For the latest qualification details, always check WJEC and <a href="https://qualifications.wales/information-support/learners-parents-carers/qualifications/pre-16-qualifications/gcses/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qualifications Wales</a> rather than assuming older GCSE papers apply to every cohort.</div>''','toc':[('check','Check the qualification'),('baseline','Find the earliest gap'),('revision','Build a revision routine'),('help','When tutoring can help')]},
{'slug':'why-gcse-physics-equations-feel-hard','title':'Why GCSE Physics Equations Feel Hard (and How to Improve)','meta':'Help for GCSE Physics students struggling with equations, units and rearranging formulae, with a clear worked example and practical revision advice.','short':'A practical explanation of how to choose, use and check Physics equations without relying on memorisation alone.','category':'Physics','image':'physics-study.webp','minutes':5,'body':'''<p>Physics can feel confusing when students know an equation but do not know when to use it. For GCSE learners, it helps to connect the physical idea, the diagram, the measured quantities and the units before substituting numbers.</p>
<h2 id="meaning">1. Give the equation physical meaning</h2><p>Consider the relationship <strong>force = mass × acceleration</strong>. Rather than starting with a triangle to memorise, ask what each quantity tells you. Mass describes how much matter the object has; acceleration tells you how quickly its velocity changes. The equation connects these to the resultant force.</p><p>A tutor can then ask how the force would change if the acceleration doubled but the mass stayed the same. That question checks understanding before any arithmetic begins.</p>
<h2 id="worked">2. Use a repeatable worked method</h2><p>Suppose a 2 kg trolley accelerates at 3 m/s². We want the resultant force.</p><ol><li>Write down the known quantities: mass = 2 kg, acceleration = 3 m/s².</li><li>Choose the relationship: force = mass × acceleration.</li><li>Substitute: force = 2 × 3.</li><li>Calculate and include the unit: <strong>6 newtons (N)</strong>.</li><li>Check whether the answer makes sense in context.</li></ol><p>This structure is more reliable than trying random arithmetic with every number in a question.</p>
<h2 id="units">3. Treat units as clues</h2><p>Units are often the overlooked reason a calculation goes wrong. Before substituting, check whether distance is in metres, time in seconds and mass in kilograms, where the chosen formula requires those units. A student who understands prefixes and conversions will find many Physics questions easier.</p><p>Try a quick verbal check: “If the answer is a speed, should I expect m/s or m²?” This builds awareness of the result rather than treating units as a decorative ending.</p>
<h2 id="practice">4. Practise choosing the equation</h2><p>Start with questions where the correct equation is obvious. Then mix two or three possible equations and ask the student to explain which quantities are given, what is required and why their choice fits. Finally, try unfamiliar contexts.</p><p>If the student cannot explain their choice, revisit the physical concept or draw a sketch. Repetition without understanding can make a familiar worksheet look easy while leaving exam problems difficult.</p>
<h2 id="tuition">5. Get support for the correct GCSE science route</h2><p>Welsh students might be taking a WJEC Physics qualification or a combined science route. The exact course affects content and question format. If you are looking for <a href="../physics-tutoring-south-wales.html">online Physics tutoring in South Wales</a>, tell us the student's year group, specification and a couple of topics they are finding difficult.</p><p>Our tutor network supports learners from Cardiff, Swansea, Newport and other South Wales communities with live one-to-one lessons.</p>''','toc':[('meaning','Understand the relationship'),('worked','Worked example'),('units','Check units'),('practice','Choose equations'),('tuition','Get suitable support')]},
{'slug':'choosing-online-tutor-cardiff-swansea-newport','title':'How to Choose an Online Tutor in Cardiff, Swansea or Newport','meta':'A practical checklist for South Wales parents choosing a GCSE or A-Level online tutor in Maths, Physics, English or Science.','short':'What to ask about subject knowledge, exam boards, fees, teaching style and tutor availability before booking.','category':'Parent guides','image':'online-learning.webp','minutes':5,'body':'''<p>When looking for a tutor in Cardiff, Swansea or Newport, it is easy to start by searching for the nearest teacher. For <strong>online tutoring</strong>, though, location is only one part of the decision. The best fit will depend on the subject, exam board, learning needs, lesson times and how clearly the tutor explains ideas.</p>
<h2 id="spec">1. Start with the subject and qualification</h2><p>“GCSE Science” covers several possible qualifications. “Maths” might refer to KS3 numeracy, WJEC GCSE Mathematics and Numeracy, GCSE resits or A-Level Maths. Be precise when you contact a provider.</p><p>For Wales, tell the provider whether the learner is studying WJEC and which specific course they are taking. New Made-for-Wales GCSEs have been phased in, so the student's year group matters.</p>
<h2 id="goals">2. Describe the actual difficulty</h2><p>Instead of only saying “needs to improve Maths”, try “understands equations in class but cannot solve unfamiliar algebra questions independently”. That gives a tutor something useful to work with. Other examples include losing marks in Physics due to unit conversions, finding it hard to write English analysis or struggling to remember Biology processes.</p>
<h2 id="style">3. Ask how the tutor teaches online</h2><p>A live lesson should be more than watching someone else answer questions. Ask whether the student will explain their thinking, receive feedback and practise during the session. A suitable tutor should be able to adapt when a concept is not understood.</p><p>Students who feel anxious about being wrong may benefit from a supportive, calm teaching style with time to think before moving on.</p>
<h2 id="fees">4. Confirm cost and arrangements</h2><p>Ask the provider for the hourly rate, how long a session lasts, the payment terms, lesson cancellation rules and what happens if a tutor is unavailable. Make sure those details are clear before the first booking.</p><p>At South Wales Tutoring, GCSE and KS3 lessons start from £20 an hour and A-Level lessons start from £25 an hour, depending on the tutor.</p>
<h2 id="local">5. Consider online convenience across South Wales</h2><p>Families in <a href="../areas/cardiff.html">Cardiff</a>, <a href="../areas/swansea.html">Swansea</a> and <a href="../areas/newport.html">Newport</a> can access a wider range of specialisms online without driving to a teaching centre. This can make it easier to find support for A-Level Physics or a particular GCSE specification.</p><p>To begin, send a short enquiry with the student’s year group, subject, exam board, goals and preferred lesson times. We can then discuss a possible match.</p>''','toc':[('spec','Check the qualification'),('goals','Describe the challenge'),('style','Ask about teaching'),('fees','Confirm fees'),('local','Think beyond location')]},
{'slug':'gcse-mock-revision-plan-south-wales','title':'GCSE Mock Revision Plan: What to Do After a Difficult Paper','meta':'A manageable GCSE mock revision plan for WJEC and other UK exam boards. Find knowledge gaps, use feedback and plan realistic practice before the next assessment.','short':'An achievable way to use mock feedback without trying to revise an entire GCSE course at once.','category':'GCSE','image':'gcse-revision.webp','minutes':5,'body':'''<p>A difficult GCSE mock can feel like a verdict, but it is more useful as information. The question is what the paper reveals about understanding, recall, method and timing, and what the student should work on next.</p>
<h2 id="mark">1. Sort mistakes into useful categories</h2><p>Look over a marked paper and divide missed marks into four groups: <strong>missing knowledge</strong>, <strong>incorrect method</strong>, <strong>question interpretation</strong> and <strong>time or checking errors</strong>. Different mistakes need different responses.</p><p>For example, a student who cannot multiply fractions needs core practice before returning to an unfamiliar algebra question that uses those skills. A student who knows the science but misreads command words may need more explanation and response structure.</p>
<h2 id="priority">2. Choose three priority topics</h2><p>Trying to revise every chapter immediately often becomes overwhelming. Select three topics that are both important to the specification and realistic to improve in the next two weeks. Start with earlier skills that unlock several later topics.</p><p>For WJEC learners, check the correct qualification and assessment materials. New GCSE courses and legacy specifications may differ between cohorts.</p>
<h2 id="routine">3. Build a repeatable weekly routine</h2><ul><li><strong>Day 1:</strong> identify one misunderstanding from the paper.</li><li><strong>Day 2:</strong> review the idea and do a few supported examples.</li><li><strong>Day 3:</strong> try similar questions independently.</li><li><strong>Day 4:</strong> mix those questions with an older topic.</li><li><strong>Day 5:</strong> practise a short timed set and mark it carefully.</li></ul><p>These are suggestions, not a rigid timetable. A short successful session is more useful than a long one in which a student cannot focus.</p>
<h2 id="paper">4. Use past papers after understanding improves</h2><p>Past papers help students practise interpretation and time management, but they are less effective when the underlying topic is not understood. Use worked examples and untimed questions to repair the concept first, then move gradually towards the paper format.</p><p>When marking, ask why an answer gained or lost marks. A student who understands the examiner’s expectation can make more purposeful improvements.</p>
<h2 id="help">5. Get help when a gap is difficult to identify</h2><p>A <a href="../gcse-tutoring-south-wales.html">one-to-one GCSE tutor</a> can provide an outside perspective and guide the learner towards the most useful next step. In South Wales, online tutoring is available to enquire about across Cardiff, Swansea, Newport, Bridgend, Caerphilly and neighbouring towns.</p><p>Share the mock paper or teacher’s topic feedback where appropriate. That often gives the tutor a better starting point than a general request to “revise everything”.</p>''','toc':[('mark','Categorise mistakes'),('priority','Choose priorities'),('routine','Build a routine'),('paper','Use past papers'),('help','When tutoring helps')]},
]
